      "narrative": "The secondary corporate debt and specialized private credit markets have issued an unmistakable warning to the artificial intelligence infrastructure boom. Specialized neo-cloud providers and GPU datacenter operators—headlined by CoreWeave, Lambda Labs, and Crusoe Energy—are confronting a severe surge in their borrowing costs. Secondary market trading data revealed that yields on specialized asset-backed debt and private credit facilities issued by CoreWeave and peer datacenter operators spiked past 16%, compared to 8.5% to 10% just twelve months ago. \n\nThis yield expansion reflects mounting institutional investor anxiety regarding the depreciation curves, technological obsolescence, and revenue sustainability of AI specialized cloud infrastructure. To finance the purchase of hundreds of thousands of Nvidia Hopper (H100/H200) and Blackwell GPUs, neo-clouds utilized debt structures known as \"GPU collateralized asset-backed loans\" (ABLs), where the physical microchips themselves were pledged as primary loan collateral. However, as enterprise AI adoption pivots from capital-intensive model pre-training toward cheaper model inference, and as major tech firms release custom internal silicon (such as Google TPU v6 and AWS Trainium), institutional lenders fear that three-year-old GPUs will suffer catastrophic residual value degradation.\n\nSimultaneously, enterprise spending telemetry published by corporate spend management platform Ramp reveals that corporate AI software budgets have plateaued across non-tech enterprises. After twelve months of unrestricted developer experimentation, Chief Information Officers (CIOs) and Chief Financial Officers (CFOs) have instituted strict FinOps token governance. Rather than paying $20 to $40 per user per month for experimental AI copilot add-ons with negligible employee adoption, enterprise IT departments are aggressively pruning seats, consolidating vendors, and demanding deterministic ROI metrics before signing multi-year contract renewals.\n\nThis tightening credit and procurement environment marks the end of the \"blank check\" phase of the generative AI revolution. Datacenter operators can no longer rely on cheap speculative debt to fund massive server clusters without signed, multi-year take-or-pay enterprise contracts. Enterprise technology executives who built architectures dependent on subsidized neo-cloud compute must immediately recalibrate their FinOps playbooks, transitioning toward quantized open-weights models, dynamic multi-cloud routing, and rigorous inference token budgeting.",
